          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Henry driveway sealer coal tar or asphalt?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Asphalt. All four residential sealers are water-based asphalt emulsions, and the safety data sheets list asphalt, water and mineral fillers with no coal tar. That makes them usable in areas that restrict coal tar sealcoat. Expect emulsion-sealer service life rather than coal tar's, and do not mix Henry products with coal tar or solvent-based sealers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does a pail of Henry sealer cover?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Henry quotes 200 to 500 square feet per pail for the 130 and 175 and 250 to 500 for the 200 and 532, depending on surface texture. The low figure applies to weathered, unsealed or rough asphalt and the high figure to smooth, recently sealed surfaces. Most driveways that need sealing land nearer the low end, so buy accordingly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you put two coats of Henry 532?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Henry's data sheet says no: do not apply more than one coat and do not apply it too thickly, because that causes slow drying and tracking. The 200 E-Z Stir carries the same instruction. If your driveway is so porous that one coat looks thin, the answer is better crack filling and a careful single coat."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long before you can drive on Henry sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Henry's data sheets put dry-to-touch at about four hours. The 175 can be driven on after 24 hours and the 200 after 24 to 48 hours under normal conditions. Cool nights, humidity and shade lengthen that. Keep cars off longer than the minimum if the coating still feels soft or shows prints when pressed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What temperature do you need to apply Henry driveway sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "At least 50°F and rising, with no rain, frost or sub-50°F temperatures forecast for 24 hours (130 and 175) or 48 hours (200 and 532). On hot days Henry recommends dampening the surface first and sweeping off puddles. Starting early in the day gives the coating the full afternoon's warmth to dry."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
