    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Henry's driveway sealers are all water-based asphalt emulsions, and the number of years printed on the pail is a product-replacement warranty, not a promise about how long the coating will last. Once you know that, choosing between the four comes down to how rough your asphalt is, and the data sheets tell you more than the labels do.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="the-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Who Makes Henry, and What It Sells for Driveways
          </h2>

          <p>
            Henry is better known for roof coatings, roof cement and foundation waterproofing than for driveways, and that is worth knowing before you go shopping, because a store aisle can put a fibered roof coating beside a driveway pail in near-identical packaging. The company's technical data sheets list its address in El Segundo, California. Since September 2021 it has been part of Carlisle Companies, which bought Henry for $1.575 billion to strengthen its building-envelope business. Driveway products are a small corner of a much larger roofing and waterproofing catalog.
          </p>

          <p>
            The residential driveway range is short. Henry's current retail catalog lists four sealers (130 Driveway Asphalt Coating, 175 Driveway Asphalt Filler & Sealer, 200 Driveway E-Z Stir Asphalt Filler & Sealer and 532 Driveway Asphalt Resurfacer) and three repair products: 304 Pothole Patch, 305 Elastomeric Crack Filler and 308 Elastomeric Crack Repair. Henry also sells commercial paving products such as HE127 Asphalt Emulsion Sealer and an acrylic additive called Duratuff, but those are contractor materials and not what you will find in a home center.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every one of the four consumer sealers is an asphalt emulsion: petroleum asphalt suspended in water with mineral fillers. The safety data sheets for the 200 and 532 list water, asphalt, quartz, slate, kaolin and bentonite, and no coal tar. That matters if you live in one of the states or towns that restrict coal tar sealcoat, because Henry's range is legal to use there. It also means you should expect emulsion-sealer lifespans rather than the longer service life coal tar products are known for. Our <a href="/coal-tar-vs-asphalt-emulsion-sealer/">coal tar vs asphalt emulsion guide</a> covers that trade-off in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no Henry acrylic driveway sealer for homeowners in the current lineup. If you were searching for one, you are probably thinking of a different brand or of Henry's commercial additive. Acrylic and asphalt emulsion sealers behave differently on the driveway and are not interchangeable, which our <a href="/acrylic-vs-asphalt-emulsion-driveway-sealer/">acrylic vs asphalt emulsion comparison</a> explains.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you came here deciding between Henry and the other big-box brand, our <a href="/latex-ite-vs-henry-driveway-sealer/">Latex-ite vs Henry comparison</a> handles that head to head. This review stays inside the Henry range: what each pail is, what the warranty actually promises, and which one fits which driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Four Henry Sealers, Side by Side
          </h2>

          <p>
            The four pails look almost identical on the shelf, and the front labels do not help much. The technical data sheets do. They list solids by weight, maximum VOC and the conditions each product is designed for. The table below combines Henry's current retail catalog (warranty, coverage and size) with the published data sheets (solids and VOC). Note that some data sheets are several years old, so the current label always takes priority.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Warranty</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coverage per pail</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Solids by weight</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Max VOC</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">130 Driveway Asphalt Coating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-500 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 g/L</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">175 Asphalt Filler & Sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-500 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">38.1%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g/L</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">200 E-Z Stir Filler & Sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">250-500 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">42-46%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 g/L</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">532 Driveway Asphalt Resurfacer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">250-500 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">42-46%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 g/L</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>130 Driveway Asphalt Coating</strong> is the basic sealcoat, described as a simple brush-applied product for home driveways. At 30 percent solids it leaves the least material behind once the water evaporates. Henry's data sheet warns that it is not recommended for steeply pitched driveways, where the incline combined with water or ice can make the surface slippery. It suits a flat, sound driveway that was sealed recently and just needs its colour back.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>175 Driveway Asphalt Filler & Sealer</strong> adds finely graded aggregate to the emulsion. The data sheet says that improves traction and appearance and fills surface cracks up to 1/8 inch, and that properly applied it will not flake, peel, track or crack. At 38 percent solids it carries noticeably more material than the 130. It is the step up for a driveway that is weathered but not rough.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>200 Driveway E-Z Stir Asphalt Filler & Sealer</strong> is the one most people mean by a Henry driveway sealer. Henry describes it as easy to stir, reinforced for traction and durability, and suited to asphalt with minor cracks up to 1/8 inch. <strong>532 Driveway Asphalt Resurfacer</strong> is the top of the range, sold as a 7X rubberized, easy-stir gel formula that fills and seals rough surfaces and cracks. Those last two are close enough that they get their own section below.
          </p>

          <p>
            One detail to read carefully is pail size. Henry's product pages say 5 gallons, while the current retail catalog and many store listings say 4.75 gallons. Coverage is quoted per pail either way, so plan on the smaller figure when you work out quantities. Our <a href="/driveway-sealer-calculator/">driveway sealer calculator</a> lets you plug in the low end of Henry's range rather than the optimistic top.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What the Warranty Years Actually Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Henry markets its sealers by warranty length, and it is easy to read a 4-year pail as a sealer that lasts four years. The warranty text does not say that. The limited warranty on each data sheet says Henry warrants only that the product is free of defects, because weather, workmanship, equipment and the condition of the driveway are outside its control. If the product is proved defective within the stated number of years from purchase, Henry will replace the product at no charge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three conditions follow from that wording. You need proof of purchase. The product must have been applied according to Henry's written directions, which include the temperature, weather and one-coat rules covered below. And the remedy is replacement material only, with no labour and no consequential damages. If the coating wears through in your tire paths after two seasons because the driveway is busy, that is wear rather than a defect, and the warranty is unlikely to help.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Read the warranty as a ranking, not a lifespan.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The years tell you how Henry ranks its own products, 130 at the bottom and 532 at the top. They do not tell you when you will be resealing. Film thickness, traffic and preparation decide that.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is there a Henry 5-year driveway sealer?</h3>

          <p>
            Not in the current lineup. Henry's catalog has 2-, 3-, 4- and 7-year products and nothing at five years. People searching for a Henry 5-year sealer are usually remembering a different brand's 5-year pail on the same shelf, or an older Henry product that has been renamed. The closest equivalents are the 4-year 200 E-Z Stir and the 7-year 532 Resurfacer. Pick between them on the condition of your driveway rather than on the number.
          </p>

          <p>
            How often you will actually reseal is a separate question, and it depends more on your driveway than on the brand. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-driveway-sealer-last/">how long driveway sealer lasts</a> breaks that down by traffic, climate and sealer type, and it is a better planning guide than any figure on the pail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="200-vs-532" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Henry 200 E-Z Stir vs 532 Resurfacer
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the decision most Henry buyers face, and the data sheets make it more interesting than the price gap suggests. The published physical properties for the 200 and the 532 are the same: 42 to 46 percent solids by weight, a Brookfield viscosity of 5,000 to 7,500 cPs, 50 g/L maximum VOC, dry to the touch in four hours at 68°F and 50 percent humidity, and the same application temperature. Their safety data sheets list the same ingredients in the same percentage ranges.
          </p>

          <p>
            The differences Henry points to are the rubber content and the job each is meant for. The 532 is sold as 7X rubberized for longer life and as a resurfacer for rough surfaces. The 200 is described as rubberized and reinforced for traction, aimed at asphalt with minor cracks. The warranty is the clearest difference, seven years against four. We cannot see the exact formula of either, so treat the 532 as Henry's stated premium version of the same family rather than a different kind of product.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, buy the 532 if your asphalt has gone rough and open-textured, with exposed stone and a surface that soaks up water. That is the surface it is designed to fill. Buy the 200 if the drive is reasonably smooth with hairline cracking and you are maintaining it rather than rescuing it. If the surface is raveling, losing stone or breaking up at the edges, neither is the answer, and our <a href="/driveway-sealer-vs-resurfacer/">sealer vs resurfacer guide</a> explains how to tell when a driveway is past coating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">One coat, not two</h3>

          <p>
            Both data sheets carry an instruction that surprises people: do not apply more than one coat, and do not apply too thickly, because extra material dries slowly and causes tracking. That is different from the general advice to put down two thin coats, which suits many other sealers, including some covered in our <a href="/how-many-coats-of-driveway-sealer/">how many coats guide</a>. With the 200 and 532 specifically, follow Henry's label. The warranty depends on applying the product as directed, and a second coat that tracks into the garage is not something Henry will cover.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 130 and 175 data sheets are less strict. They warn against applying too thickly but do not set a one-coat limit. If you want to build film thickness on a porous driveway with two passes, those are the Henry products that allow it. Most owners with a rough driveway will get further with a single, well-worked coat of the 532 than with two coats of a thinner pail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="crack-repair" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Henry's Crack Filler and Pothole Patch
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every Henry sealer data sheet says the same thing about preparation: repair cracks and holes first, and let the repairs cure before you seal. Sealers that fill cracks up to 1/8 inch handle hairline crazing. Anything wider needs a dedicated filler, otherwise the sealer bridges the gap, cures and splits along the same line within a season. Henry makes three repair products for this, and they do different jobs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>305 Driveway Elastomeric Crack Filler</strong> comes in a 0.90-gallon squeeze container. Henry describes it as an elastomer-modified asphalt emulsion for cracks up to 1/2 inch wide, applied straight from the container, at 50°F and rising. The data sheet says it fills cracks up to 3/8 inch level with the surface. Wider cracks should be filled slightly high to allow for shrinkage, and deep cracks should be packed with sand to within 1/4 inch of the top first. It also suggests sprinkling sand over the filler to reduce tracking while it dries.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>308 Elastomeric Crack Repair</strong> is a thick paste in a 10.1-ounce caulk cartridge, rated for cracks up to 1/2 inch. Henry quotes about 15 lineal feet of 3/8-inch bead per cartridge, which helps you work out how many tubes a cracked driveway needs. For cracks deeper than an inch, fill with clean sand to within an inch of the surface. Allow at least 24 hours in good weather before you seal over it. A cartridge gives more control than a squeeze jug, and that matters along a garage apron where neat lines show.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>304 Driveway Asphalt Pothole Patch</strong> is a ready-to-use asphalt and aggregate cold patch. The current data sheet quotes roughly 2 square feet at 1 inch deep per 11-pound container. Holes deeper than 2 inches should go in layers no thicker than 2 inches, each tamped down. It can take traffic immediately after compacting, but Henry says to let it cure for three weeks before sealing. Older Henry sealer data sheets said 90 days, so if your patch is in a shaded or cool spot, waiting longer does no harm.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need more than a few tubes, our <a href="/best-asphalt-crack-filler/">asphalt crack filler rankings</a> compare Henry's fillers with the hot-pour and rope options for wide or numerous cracks. Our guide on <a href="/fill-driveway-cracks-before-sealing/">filling driveway cracks before sealing</a> covers timing so the repairs and the sealcoat do not end up fighting each other.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Application Rules From Henry's Data Sheets
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most failed sealcoats come down to weather or preparation rather than the product, and Henry's directions are specific. The minimum is 50°F, and the 200 and 532 data sheets say 50°F and rising. Do not apply if temperatures below 50°F, rain or frost are forecast. The 130 and 175 sheets give a 24-hour window, and the 200 and 532 sheets ask for 48 hours. On hot days, Henry recommends dampening the surface first and sweeping away any puddles. Our <a href="/what-temperature-to-seal-driveway/">driveway sealing temperature guide</a> explains why the overnight low matters as much as the afternoon high.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are three places these products should not go. The data sheets say not to use them on concrete, and not on asphalt less than six months old, which still needs to release oils before it can hold a coating. The 200 and 532 sheets also say not to mix them with a coal tar emulsion or a solvent-based sealer. If you have a new driveway, our <a href="/when-should-you-seal-a-new-asphalt-driveway/">guide to sealing new asphalt</a> covers the waiting period.
          </p>

          <p>
            Preparation follows the usual order. Sweep off loose stone, rinse away dust with a hose, and scrub grease and oil spots with a driveway cleaner or detergent. Then make crack and pothole repairs and let them cure. Our <a href="/how-to-clean-asphalt-driveway-before-sealing/">cleaning guide</a> goes through each step. Oil stains are the most common reason an emulsion sealer fails to bond in patches, so do not skip the scrubbing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixing is where the 200 and 532 earn their easy-stir name, but they still need proper mixing. The 175 and 130 data sheets call for five to ten minutes with a wooden paddle until the sealer is smooth and uniform, and the 200 sheet says to mix in a circular, bottom-to-top motion. The material can look brown in the pail and dries to black. Apply with the brush side of a squeegee, work in 3- to 4-foot sections in a cross pattern so every pore is filled, and pull out ridges as you go. Do not thin any of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect the surface to be dry to the touch in about four hours. The 175 sheet says it can be driven on in 24 hours under normal conditions, and the 200 sheet says 24 to 48 hours. Cool, humid or shaded conditions push those times out. Our <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/">drying time guide</a> covers what slows a cure. Wet sealer cleans up with water, while dried material needs mineral spirits or, per the newer sheets, a terpene-based solvent.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">From Henry's Data Sheets</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50°F+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum application temperature</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">4 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Dry to touch (200, 532, 175)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24-48 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Before driving (200)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">6 months</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum age of new asphalt</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Owners Report, and Our Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Henry does not have the volume of long-term owner write-ups you might expect from such a common brand. Most of what is online is short retailer reviews written soon after application. Within that limited record, the themes are reasonably clear. In owner forum threads, people who had used a cheaper store-brand pail have said they would pay a little more for Henry next time, and one buyer's one-year report on the E-Z Stir said it still looked freshly applied in full sun. The complaints we found are mostly about weather. One crack filler reviewer found it vulnerable to rain even after the 24-hour drying period and advised waiting for a dry forecast of three or four days.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matches what the data sheets would predict. These are consumer emulsion sealers. They do well on a clean, repaired driveway sealed in warm, dry weather, and they disappoint when applied over dust, oil or open cracks, or put down too thickly. No independent long-term durability data exists for any of the four that we could find, and we have not tested them ourselves, so plan your resealing schedule around your own driveway rather than the pail.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Your driveway</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Henry product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Recently sealed, smooth, flat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">130 or 175</td>
