                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Our verdict: for a typical suburban asphalt driveway that has been sealed before and has some hairline cracking, the 200 E-Z Stir is the Henry pail to buy. If the surface has gone rough and open, step up to the 532 and apply one careful coat. The 130 and 175 are reasonable for a recently sealed, smooth driveway that only needs its colour back. Whichever you choose, the 305 or 308 crack filler matters as much as the pail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Henry entirely if your driveway is concrete, because none of these products is for concrete. A penetrating sealer such as the silane-siloxane products in our <a href="/best-driveway-sealer/">best driveway sealer guide</a> is the right category, and our <a href="/masonry-defender-review/">Masonry Defender review</a> covers one concrete option. For other asphalt choices, our <a href="/latex-ite-driveway-sealer-review/">Latex-ite review</a> and our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">asphalt sealer rankings</a> include the contractor-grade products that neither big-box brand competes with.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the Warranty as a Lifespan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 4 or 7 years on the pail is a product-defect replacement promise that requires proof of purchase and correct application. It covers replacement material only, not labour, and it says nothing about how long the coating will last on your driveway's tire paths.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting Two Coats of 200 or 532 Down</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Henry's data sheets for both say not to apply more than one coat or apply too thickly, because the extra material dries slowly and tracks. With these two products, one well-worked coat is the instruction, and a tracked second coat may not be covered.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over Open Cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 200 and 175 fill cracks up to 1/8 inch. Anything wider needs 305 or 308 crack filler first, cured for at least 24 hours. Otherwise the sealer bridges the crack and splits along the same line within a season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the 48-Hour Forecast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 200 and 532 sheets ask for no rain, frost or sub-50°F temperatures within 48 hours, which is stricter than many owners assume. Sealing on a marginal day is the most common cause of a coat that washes, streaks or stays soft.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using It on Concrete or New Asphalt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every Henry driveway sealer data sheet says not to apply it to concrete or to asphalt less than six months old. Concrete needs a penetrating sealer, and new asphalt needs time to cure before any emulsion coating can bond.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Henry driveway sealer any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a consumer asphalt emulsion, yes, as long as you match the pail to the driveway. The 200 E-Z Stir suits most maintained driveways and the 532 Resurfacer suits rough ones. Results depend heavily on cleaning, crack repair and weather, so preparation matters more than the brand name once you are in this price tier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Henry make a 5-year driveway sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not currently. Henry's retail catalog lists sealers with 2-, 3-, 4- and 7-year limited warranties: 130, 175, 200 E-Z Stir and 532 Resurfacer. A 5-year pail on the same shelf is usually another brand. If you want something between Henry's middle and top products, choose the 200 or the 532 based on how rough your driveway is.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Henry driveway sealer coal tar or asphalt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Asphalt. All four residential sealers are water-based asphalt emulsions, and the safety data sheets list asphalt, water and mineral fillers with no coal tar. That makes them usable in areas that restrict coal tar sealcoat. Expect emulsion-sealer service life rather than coal tar's, and do not mix Henry products with coal tar or solvent-based sealers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a pail of Henry sealer cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Henry quotes 200 to 500 square feet per pail for the 130 and 175 and 250 to 500 for the 200 and 532, depending on surface texture. The low figure applies to weathered, unsealed or rough asphalt and the high figure to smooth, recently sealed surfaces. Most driveways that need sealing land nearer the low end, so buy accordingly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put two coats of Henry 532?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Henry's data sheet says no: do not apply more than one coat and do not apply it too thickly, because that causes slow drying and tracking. The 200 E-Z Stir carries the same instruction. If your driveway is so porous that one coat looks thin, the answer is better crack filling and a careful single coat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before you can drive on Henry sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Henry's data sheets put dry-to-touch at about four hours. The 175 can be driven on after 24 hours and the 200 after 24 to 48 hours under normal conditions. Cool nights, humidity and shade lengthen that. Keep cars off longer than the minimum if the coating still feels soft or shows prints when pressed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What temperature do you need to apply Henry driveway sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least 50°F and rising, with no rain, frost or sub-50°F temperatures forecast for 24 hours (130 and 175) or 48 hours (200 and 532). On hot days Henry recommends dampening the surface first and sweeping off puddles. Starting early in the day gives the coating the full afternoon's warmth to dry.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

